Equivalence Groups Found
├── Modbus TCP client for reading/writing holding registers on industrial field devices
│   ├── Candidates : pymodbus, modbus-tk
│   ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, connect, read_holding_registers, write_register, isError, registers
│   ├── Pitfalls   : modbus-tk uses a different object model (modbus_tcp.TcpMaster + master.execute with function codes) instead of pymodbus's 
│   │   ModbusTcpClient.read_holding_registers/write_register calls; Error handling differs: pymodbus returns a response object with isError(), modbus-tk 
│   │   raises exceptions on Modbus errors; Unit/slave id handling and connection lifecycle (connect/close) differ between libraries; Register addressing 
│   │   conventions and function code mapping must be verified to match (0-based vs 1-based)
│   ├── Constraints: requires_connector
│   └── Both pymodbus and modbus-tk provide Modbus TCP master/client implementations capable of reading/writing holding registers, which is the exact operation
│       performed here (_read1/write_valve). modbus-tk exposes a comparable master.execute() API for TCP masters and could replace pymodbus's ModbusTcpClient 
│       for the same functional role of talking to a Modbus TCP slave.
└── Modbus client library substitution (TCP master -> serial-oriented alternative)
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, read_holding_registers, write_register, isError
    ├── Pitfalls   : minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial RTU communication (RS-485/RS-232); using it for TCP requires a serial-to-TCP bridge or a 
    │   different transport class, which is a nontrivial architectural change; minimalmodbus's read_register/write_register calls return raw values directly 
    │   and raise exceptions on error instead of returning response objects with isError(); No direct equivalent of pymodbus's unit= parameter semantics; 
    │   minimalmodbus configures slave address on the instrument object itself; Different connection/instantiation model (Instrument(port, slaveaddress) vs 
    │   ModbusTcpClient(host, port) + connect())
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The file's own docstring explicitly names minimalmodbus as a considered substitution target for pymodbus. Both are used to read/write Modbus holding 
        registers for sensor/actuator I/O, matching the functional role of read_temperature/read_pressure/write_valve.

